# Cloudflare Pages headers. Applied at the edge from the ROOT of the uploaded
# directory only — deploy.yml assembles `_site/` from prod-src/site/, so this
# file is the root one and its rules cover the /dev/ lane too. (A copy also
# lands at _site/dev/_headers from main; Cloudflare ignores nested ones.)
# GitHub Pages ignores this file entirely, which is fine — it is on its way out.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On 2026-08-10 account-modal.js was shipped with a syntax error that broke the
# community and clubs pages. The fix was deployed and verified with curl — and
# the browser kept running the broken file anyway.
#
# The default here was `Cache-Control: public, max-age=14400`: a FOUR HOUR
# browser cache. `must-revalidate` does not help, because it only applies once
# max-age has expired; inside the window the browser never asks. So a corrected
# file was invisible for four hours to every device that had already loaded the
# broken one, and the site looked unfixed long after it was fixed.
#
# ⚠️ THE KEY FACT: none of this site's assets are content-hashed. `identity.js`,
# `account-modal.js`, `reviews.js` and the JSON payloads all keep the same
# filename forever and change their bytes in place. That is the exact opposite
# of the situation where a long cache is safe. `immutable`, and the year-long
# max-age used for hashed bundles in library_catalog, would be a lie here — the
# URL genuinely does not change when the content does.
#
# So: never trust a cached copy of anything whose name is stable.
#
# The cost is one conditional request per file per load. Cloudflare serves these
# with an ETag, so an unchanged file answers 304 with no body. That is a
# round trip, not a download, and it buys the guarantee that a deploy is a
# deploy rather than a suggestion.
#
# Covers are deliberately NOT listed. They are large, one per book, and replaced
# almost never — they keep the default and should. This file is about
# correctness of code and data, not about image bandwidth.
#
# If hashed filenames ever arrive (a build step, a bundler), THOSE may be
# `immutable` with a long max-age, and should be — see
# library_catalog/apps/web/public/_headers, which does exactly that because Vite
# hashes its bundles.

# ⚠️ Extension patterns like `/*.js` DO NOT WORK here. The first version of this
# file used them and measured no effect: identity.js still came back
# `max-age=14400` after deploying. Cloudflare's `*` is a path splat, not a glob,
# so a wildcard has to be a trailing segment. `/*` and `/covers/*` are the shapes
# that match. (The `no-cache` seen on .html was Pages' own default for HTML, not
# a rule of ours — easy to misread as the pattern working.)
#
# So: catch everything, then hand the images their cache back. Later rules win
# for the same header name, which is why the override sits below the catch-all
# and not above it. A new .js file added tomorrow is covered without anyone
# remembering to list it, which an explicit filename list would not manage.

/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/covers/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/dev/covers/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/static/img/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE READER'S CSP — viewer phase 1b, 2026-08-17
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /read is the FIRST page on this origin that wants a Content-Security-Policy,
# and the reason is specific: it handles a large binary from a CREDENTIALED
# endpoint, holding a Firebase ID token, and hands it to a vendored renderer.
# Nothing else here has that shape. The rest of the site still ships no CSP;
# widening that is a separate decision, not a side effect of this one.
#
# ⚠️ BOTH LANES AND BOTH PATH FORMS ARE LISTED, and each line earns its place:
#   * `/dev/read` — the dev lane is a PATH, not a host, so a `/read` rule does
#     NOT cover it. Reviewing on the dev lane with no CSP would mean the
#     header's first real exercise is in production.
#   * `/read/` — the trailing-slash 308 trap. Cloudflare redirects, and a rule
#     matching only the canonical form leaves the redirected request bare.
# Cloudflare's `*` is a path splat, not a glob (see the note above), so these
# have to be spelled out rather than written as one pattern.
#
# ⚠️ WHAT EACH DIRECTIVE IS FOR — do not trim one without reading its reason:
#   script-src   'self' plus gstatic/apis.google.com for the Firebase SDK,
#                exactly the estate's posture. ⚠️ NO 'unsafe-inline': this is
#                why site/reader.js is an external module and must stay one.
#                ⚠️ NO 'unsafe-eval' either — pdf.js is given
#                `isEvalSupported: false` so it never asks. Turning the flag
#                off beats widening the header.
#   connect-src  the gated Worker (the manifest AND every range request), plus
#                Google's token endpoints, plus ⚠️ 'self'. If this is wrong the
#                fetch dies as an OPAQUE network error, indistinguishable from
#                the Worker being down — the exact misdiagnosis the estate
#                already ate once when a CSP silently blocked a subdomain.
#                ⚠️ 'self' IS NOT PADDING, and it was MISSING until it was
#                caught by exercising this exact policy string in a browser
#                (2026-08-17). `default-src 'none'` blocks same-origin fetches
#                too, and pdf.js fetches its cMaps and standard fonts with
#                fetch() — so without 'self' a CJK page renders as BOXES and a
#                page using a non-embedded base-14 font renders with NO TEXT.
#                Both look like a corrupt book rather than a blocked request,
#                and neither would have appeared on the /dev/ lane, because the
#                root _headers comes from the PROD branch (deploy.yml copies
#                prod-src/site/. to the _site root; Cloudflare ignores nested
#                ones) — so production would have been this policy's FIRST
#                exercise.
#                ⚠️ auth.heygabi.ai ADDED 2026-08-17 for the DEV-LANE CURTAIN
#                (site/dev-lane.js): it reads the caller's effective
#                `dev_access` from GET /api/estate/me before drawing the /dev/
#                reader. It is listed on ALL FOUR rules rather than only the
#                two /dev/ ones, deliberately — four policy strings that differ
#                by one token is a trap nobody would spot in review, the
#                promoted pages never make the call (the curtain is lane-aware
#                and returns before fetching), and the auth Worker's own
#                ME_ORIGINS refuses ebooks.heygabi.ai anyway. ⚠️ And note WHERE
#                this bites: the curtain is only ever exercised on /dev/, which
#                today ships NO CSP at all (§ the note above) — so a missing
#                entry here would first break AT THE PROMOTE, as a curtain that
#                silently stopped curtaining.
#   worker-src   pdf.js runs its parser on a real Worker thread, self-hosted.
#                blob: is kept because pdf.js falls back to a blob worker when
#                a direct module worker is refused.
#   img-src      blob: and data: are how a renderer materialises extracted
#                images. ⚠️ Omitting blob: gives a reader that loads,
#                paginates and shows NO pictures — a failure that looks like a
#                data problem. The R2 covers host is named for the shelf's art.
#   frame-src /
#   child-src    blob:, for viewer phase 2 — an EPUB renderer draws into an
#                iframe it creates. Present now so the EPUB build does not
#                have to touch this file and re-verify it.
#   style-src /
#   font-src     ⚠️ blob: ADDED at viewer phase 2 (2026-08-17), and the phase-1
#                note above ("the EPUB build does not have to touch this file")
#                was WRONG by exactly these two directives. foliate-js rewrites
#                an EPUB's own stylesheets and embedded fonts to blob: URLs, and
#                `'self'` DOES NOT COVER blob: — measured, not reasoned, by
#                serving both policy strings to a real book:
#                  style-src 'self'        -> the linked sheet yields ZERO rules
#                                             and body font is Times New Roman
#                  style-src 'self' blob:  -> 84 rules, body font Palatino
#                A blocked font-src blob: was caught the same way (an explicit
#                `securitypolicyviolation: font-src <- blob`).
#                ⚠️ THE FAILURE IS SILENT AND LOOKS LIKE A BAD BOOK, not like a
#                blocked request: the reader opens, paginates and turns pages
#                perfectly, in the browser's default serif, with every typeface
#                and drop-cap the publisher chose thrown away. And ⚠️ the page's
#                own `securitypolicyviolation` listener NEVER HEARS IT — the
#                section is a blob: iframe inside a CLOSED shadow root, so the
#                violation fires on that document, not on this one.
#                ⚠️ `link.sheet` being non-null is NOT proof the CSS applied. A
#                blocked stylesheet still gets a CSSStyleSheet object; it just
#                has no rules in it. Count `sheet.cssRules.length`.
#   base-uri /
#   form-action /
#   frame-ancestors  ⚠️ 'self', AND IT WAS 'none' UNTIL 2026-08-18, WHEN 'none'
#                TURNED OUT TO BE THE P1 BLANK-READER BUG ON EVERY WEBKIT
#                BROWSER. This is the single most surprising line in the file,
#                so it gets the full story.
#
#                THE SYMPTOM. iPhone Safari, PROD /read, signed in, a perfectly
#                ordinary reflowable EPUB: the whole page chrome renders —
#                masthead, resolved title, the Page control, the arrows, the
#                footer — and the book area is an EMPTY BORDERED BOX. No error
#                anywhere, no gate card, nothing in the UI at all. Desktop
#                Chrome renders the same book flawlessly.
#
#                WHY. foliate-js paints each section into an iframe whose `src`
#                is a `blob:` URL it makes itself (site/static/foliate/
#                paginator.js, `View.load()`). A blob: document INHERITS the
#                creating document's CSP — including this `frame-ancestors`.
#                WebKit then enforces it on that child, and 'none' means "no
#                page may frame me", so the reader's own page is refused the
#                right to frame its own blob:
#                  Refused to load blob:... because it does not appear in the
#                  frame-ancestors directive of the Content Security Policy.
#                ⚠️ CHROMIUM DOES NOT DO THIS. Same policy string, same book,
#                same commit: Chromium renders, WebKit does not. That is why
#                every desktop review passed and the first phone killed it.
#
#                ⚠️ AND WHY IT IS SILENT rather than an error, which is the
#                half that cost the time. `View.load()` is
#                `new Promise(resolve => { iframe.addEventListener('load', ...);
#                iframe.src = src })` — the promise resolves ONLY from inside
#                that listener. The blocked frame still fires `load`, but as an
#                opaque sandboxed document, so `iframe.contentDocument` is null
#                and `afterLoad(doc)` throws `TypeError: null is not an object
#                (evaluating 'doc.head')` INSIDE THE LISTENER. The throw escapes
#                to window.onerror; the promise is never resolved and never
#                rejected. `view.init()` hangs forever. reader.js's catch never
#                runs, `closed()` is never called, and the reader waits for a
#                page that is never coming. (site/reader.js now has a watchdog
#                and a window-level error panel precisely for that shape.)
#
#                MEASURED 2026-08-18, one variable at a time, Playwright WebKit
#                (iPhone 13 profile) against this exact policy string and the
#                real book "All The Skills - 5":
#                  frame-ancestors 'none'   -> blank box, view.init() never
#                                              settles, 45s timeout
#                  frame-ancestors 'self'   -> renders, relocate at 3%
#                  no frame-ancestors line  -> renders, relocate at 3%
#                Everything else in the policy was held constant and is fine.
#
#                ⚠️ 'self' IS NOT A WEAKENING WORTH WORRYING ABOUT, and 'none'
#                must not be "restored for safety". The threat frame-ancestors
#                answers is CLICKJACKING BY ANOTHER SITE, and 'self' refuses
#                every other origin exactly as 'none' did. All 'self' adds is
#                the reader framing its own same-origin blob:, which is the
#                renderer doing its job. `frame-ancestors 'none'` here does not
#                buy one unit of security; it buys a book that will not open in
#                Safari, on an iPad, and in Chrome and Firefox on iOS (all of
#                which are WebKit).
#
#                This page still submits nothing and has no base tag, so
#                form-action/base-uri stay 'none'.
/read
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' https://www.gstatic.com https://apis.google.com; connect-src 'self' https://audiobook-api.heygabi.ai https://auth.heygabi.ai https://identitytoolkit.googleapis.com https://securetoken.googleapis.com https://firestore.googleapis.com https://www.googleapis.com; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://covers.heygabi.ai; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' blob:; font-src 'self' data: blob:; worker-src 'self' blob:; frame-src 'self' blob: https://audiobook-catalog.firebaseapp.com; child-src 'self' blob:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'self'

/read/
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' https://www.gstatic.com https://apis.google.com; connect-src 'self' https://audiobook-api.heygabi.ai https://auth.heygabi.ai https://identitytoolkit.googleapis.com https://securetoken.googleapis.com https://firestore.googleapis.com https://www.googleapis.com; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://covers.heygabi.ai; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' blob:; font-src 'self' data: blob:; worker-src 'self' blob:; frame-src 'self' blob: https://audiobook-catalog.firebaseapp.com; child-src 'self' blob:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'self'

